WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:08.760
All right, so now before we go to school and start practicing, I would like to show you the syntax

00:08.760 --> 00:10.080
of the index.

00:10.080 --> 00:11.440
So it's very very simple.

00:11.440 --> 00:13.360
It starts with create.

00:13.400 --> 00:17.400
And then we can define whether it is clustered or non-clustered.

00:17.400 --> 00:19.240
And then the keyword index.

00:19.240 --> 00:21.280
But this section is optional.

00:21.280 --> 00:25.520
So if you don't define anything the default can be the non-clustered.

00:25.520 --> 00:31.080
So if you say create index the SQL server going to go and create non-clustered index.

00:31.080 --> 00:34.440
Then after that we have to go and define the name of the index.

00:34.440 --> 00:40.000
And then we have to tell SQL which table we have to create the index in on table name.

00:40.000 --> 00:45.240
And then we can go and define one column or multiple columns for the index.

00:45.240 --> 00:49.400
And we call an index with multiple columns as composite index.

00:49.400 --> 00:53.680
So for example we can go and create a clustered index using this command.

00:53.720 --> 00:56.280
Create clustered index the index name.

00:56.280 --> 00:59.700
And then we specify the table and the ID.

00:59.700 --> 01:03.020
So we are saying create clustered index based on this column.

01:03.060 --> 01:04.940
The ID from the table customers.

01:04.940 --> 01:07.820
And if you want to create a non-clustered index you say.

01:07.860 --> 01:09.700
Create non-clustered index.

01:09.860 --> 01:10.860
And the same thing.

01:10.860 --> 01:12.780
So so far we are using one column in the.

01:12.820 --> 01:13.180
Index.

01:13.180 --> 01:17.580
But we can go and create a composite index with multiple columns like the following.

01:17.620 --> 01:18.140
Example.

01:18.140 --> 01:19.780
So we can say create an index.

01:19.780 --> 01:22.820
And as you can see we skipped here defining the type.

01:22.860 --> 01:26.740
And that's because the default is going to be non-clustered index.

01:26.740 --> 01:28.140
And now here we are specifying.

01:28.180 --> 01:30.860
Two columns the last name and the first name.

01:30.860 --> 01:33.220
And as you can see we are specifying as well for.

01:33.260 --> 01:35.500
SQL how to sort the data.

01:35.500 --> 01:40.500
So we are saying LastName should be sorted inside the database ascending.

01:40.540 --> 01:41.820
Lowest to the highest.

01:41.820 --> 01:46.020
But the first name should be the way around from the highest to the lowest.

01:46.100 --> 01:50.540
So you can control how the data are going to be sorted physically in the data page.

01:50.540 --> 01:51.980
So as you can see it is very simple.

01:51.980 --> 01:55.180
This is the syntax for creating index in SQL.

01:55.220 --> 01:56.580
All right so back to SQL.

01:56.580 --> 02:00.640
And the first question is where do we find indexes in the database?

02:00.720 --> 02:02.200
Well, you can go and explore it.

02:02.200 --> 02:06.840
If you go to the Object Explorer over here and check any tables from our sales DB, for example, the

02:06.840 --> 02:07.600
customers.

02:07.600 --> 02:09.840
And here you have a folder called indexes.

02:09.840 --> 02:13.360
So if you expand it you will find here an index.

02:13.360 --> 02:18.960
I didn't create any of those indexes in the database, but in SQL server, if you define any of the

02:18.960 --> 02:26.000
columns as a primary key, the SQL server is going to go by default, creating a clustered index for

02:26.000 --> 02:30.880
the primary key, because it makes always sense to create a clustered index on the primary key.

02:30.880 --> 02:32.840
So this one is created as a default.

02:32.840 --> 02:36.280
And as you can see at the start we have like a key primary key customer.

02:36.280 --> 02:38.080
And then it is clustered.

02:38.120 --> 02:39.640
Now I would like to start from the scratch.

02:39.640 --> 02:43.800
That's why I would like to go and create a new table without any indexes.

02:43.800 --> 02:48.440
So what we're going to do, we're going to go and load the table customers into a new table.

02:48.440 --> 02:49.520
So how we can do that.

02:49.520 --> 02:54.840
We're going to go and say select star from sales customers.

02:54.840 --> 02:54.980
Worse.

02:55.660 --> 02:59.580
And before the from we're going to say into a new table.

02:59.580 --> 03:02.220
So it's going to be TB customers.

03:02.740 --> 03:04.780
So like this let's go and execute it.

03:04.780 --> 03:10.300
So now if we go to the left side and refresh the tables, you can find we have now a new table called

03:10.500 --> 03:11.580
DB customers.

03:11.620 --> 03:14.500
Now let's go and check whether we have any indexes inside it.

03:14.500 --> 03:17.180
So indexes it is empty.

03:17.180 --> 03:20.340
So we don't have anything no clustered index or anything else.

03:20.340 --> 03:24.500
And this table has the structure of heap structure.

03:24.500 --> 03:26.900
So the data are inserted there randomly.

03:26.900 --> 03:28.180
It is not sorted.

03:28.180 --> 03:36.260
And if I go over here and for example let's say I'm going to select from this new table where customer

03:36.260 --> 03:39.980
ID equal one and I execute it.

03:39.980 --> 03:45.100
The SQL server did a full scan on the table in order to find this customer ID.

03:45.100 --> 03:49.380
So our new table db customers is a heap cluster.

03:49.380 --> 03:50.980
But let's go and change that.

03:50.980 --> 03:51.580
What we're going to do.

03:51.580 --> 03:54.480
We're going to go and create a new clustered index.

03:54.640 --> 03:59.440
So we're going to say create clustered index.

03:59.440 --> 04:02.200
And then we're going to go and give it a name for the index.

04:02.200 --> 04:03.920
We usually follow the following index.

04:03.920 --> 04:06.120
So we have index as prefix.

04:06.120 --> 04:08.400
And then after that we specify the table name.

04:08.400 --> 04:13.040
So db customers and then the key for the index.

04:13.040 --> 04:16.440
So the column that you are using in order to index the table.

04:16.440 --> 04:20.200
This is important to stick with the same naming convention for the index name.

04:20.200 --> 04:24.440
Because later as you are monitoring your indexes, it's going to be really easy to understand.

04:24.480 --> 04:30.720
Okay, this index is for the table DB customers and we are using the customer ID to index.

04:30.720 --> 04:34.520
So now after that we're going to go specify on which table we are doing the index.

04:34.520 --> 04:38.280
So on sales DB customers.

04:39.040 --> 04:42.400
And then we're going to specify that column name.

04:42.400 --> 04:46.920
So we are saying build for me clustered index based on the customer ID.

04:46.920 --> 04:48.760
So now let's go and execute it.

04:48.760 --> 04:54.050
So as you can see it's very fast because we have only five rows, so the database just switched all

04:54.090 --> 04:55.770
the data pages very fast.

04:55.810 --> 04:57.930
Now let's go and check our new index.

04:57.930 --> 05:00.490
So let's go and refresh and let's go inside it.

05:00.490 --> 05:05.370
And now we can see that we have our new index clustered index based on the customer ID.

05:05.410 --> 05:08.530
Now as we learned we cannot create multiple clustered index.

05:08.530 --> 05:10.090
But let's go and test that.

05:10.090 --> 05:12.130
So I will just take the whole thing.

05:12.450 --> 05:19.050
And let's say I would like to create a clustered index based on the first name as well here.

05:21.130 --> 05:23.730
So let's go and execute it.

05:23.730 --> 05:29.330
So as you can see SQL saying you cannot create more than one clustered index on this table.

05:29.330 --> 05:32.010
That means we can create only one clustered index.

05:32.250 --> 05:37.890
And let's say that after you created the index you choose the wrong column and you would like to change

05:37.890 --> 05:39.330
it to the first name.

05:39.530 --> 05:43.250
So what we're going to do, we have to go and drop the index.

05:43.250 --> 05:45.810
So we say drop index.

05:45.810 --> 05:48.010
And then you need the index name.

05:48.010 --> 05:49.450
It was this one.

05:49.450 --> 05:51.990
And then you have to specify which table.

05:52.150 --> 05:57.590
So it's going to be sales DB customers like this.

05:57.750 --> 06:04.350
So if I do it like this and let's go and refresh again you can see that we don't have any indexes anymore.

06:04.350 --> 06:06.590
And the table is backed as a heap structure.

06:06.630 --> 06:11.430
And now you can go and create the correct clustered index for this table.

06:11.470 --> 06:13.910
But to be honest I'm going to stick with the customer ID.

06:13.910 --> 06:18.790
So I will not create a clustered index on the first name, because the first name of course is not unique.

06:18.790 --> 06:22.310
You can have like maybe multiple customers having the same name.

06:22.310 --> 06:26.910
And as well updates could happen on the first name and that's going to be very expensive.

06:26.950 --> 06:30.510
So that means I'm going to stick with my index on the customer ID.

06:30.550 --> 06:31.790
Let's go and execute it.

06:31.830 --> 06:36.030
And now I have again my index on my table.

06:36.070 --> 06:41.190
Now let's say that that I have the following select statement from our tables.

06:41.510 --> 06:50.690
So customers and I'm searching for the last name Well, let's say we are searching for Brown, so let's

06:50.690 --> 06:51.570
go and execute it.

06:51.570 --> 06:55.530
So let's say that we are getting more and more customers and our table is getting bigger.

06:55.850 --> 06:58.250
And I frequently use this query.

06:58.250 --> 07:01.850
So I'm searching for specific customers using the last name.

07:01.850 --> 07:07.690
So what we can do we can go and create a non-clustered index for the last name in order to improve the

07:07.690 --> 07:09.250
performance of this query.

07:09.250 --> 07:10.490
So let's go and create that.

07:10.490 --> 07:14.810
So we're going to say create non-clustered index.

07:14.850 --> 07:17.610
And now we're going to give it the name using the naming convention.

07:17.610 --> 07:19.850
So DB customers.

07:19.850 --> 07:22.610
And we're going to use the last name for this index.

07:22.930 --> 07:31.290
So on sales DB customers and we will use the column last name for the index.

07:31.290 --> 07:33.690
So let's go and execute it.

07:33.730 --> 07:39.570
And now if you go to our indexes and refresh we will find our new index over here.

07:39.730 --> 07:44.330
And as you can see it says it is non-clustered and as well non-unique.

07:44.330 --> 07:46.390
We will talk about the uniqueness later.

07:46.390 --> 07:47.510
So as you can see, it's very easy.

07:47.550 --> 07:50.750
We have just created a non-clustered index on the last name.

07:50.790 --> 07:55.830
And now as we learned, we can go and create multiple non-clustered index on the same table.

07:56.150 --> 07:59.110
Let's say for example, now our query looks like this.

07:59.110 --> 08:03.830
We are searching for the first name using for example the value Anna.

08:03.870 --> 08:06.390
And now this query happens a lot and may be slow.

08:06.390 --> 08:10.310
So we can go and create a new non-clustered index.

08:10.310 --> 08:12.430
So let me just have it like this.

08:12.430 --> 08:17.950
And for the non-clustered index you don't have to specify always like non-clustered index as default.

08:17.950 --> 08:19.110
It's going to be non-clustered.

08:19.110 --> 08:20.670
So we can skip that.

08:20.710 --> 08:23.470
And here let's call it first name.

08:24.030 --> 08:28.030
And the column that we are using is the first name.

08:28.550 --> 08:31.590
So let's go and create this index.

08:31.630 --> 08:33.990
And now let's go and refresh our indexes.

08:34.030 --> 08:39.270
And as you can see SQL did create a non-clustered index for the first name.

08:39.270 --> 08:44.750
So if you don't specify the type of the index it's going to be as a default non-clustered index.
